## Updated deployment flow

1. Developer writes .NET code in Visual Studio.
2. Developer pushes code to GitHub.
3. Jenkins pipeline gets triggered through a webhook.
4. Jenkins restores NuGet packages, builds the .NET application and runs unit tests.
5. Jenkins builds Docker images for frontend and backend.
6. Jenkins pushes Docker images to Amazon ECR.
7. Jenkins connects to EC2 instances and deploys the latest Docker images.
8. Docker containers run on EC2 instances.
9. Application Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic.
10. Backend connects to Amazon RDS SQL Server.
11. CloudWatch monitors application logs and infrastructure.

## Updated spoken explanation

"In our project, we have implemented a three-tier .NET application and deployed it on AWS using Docker.

We have ASP.NET Core MVC for the presentation layer, ASP.NET Core Web API using .NET 8 for the business logic, and Amazon RDS SQL Server for the database.

We containerize both frontend and backend applications using Docker. We create separate Docker images for both applications.

We use Amazon ECR to store our Docker images.

For hosting, we use Amazon EC2 instances, where Docker containers are deployed and executed.

We use an AWS Application Load Balancer to distribute incoming user requests across the application instances.

For networking, we use Amazon VPC with public and private subnets. Our application servers run in private subnets, and our database is hosted in private database subnets.

For CI/CD, we use Jenkins integrated with GitHub.

Whenever a developer pushes code to GitHub, Jenkins automatically triggers the pipeline. Jenkins restores NuGet packages, builds the .NET application, runs unit tests, builds Docker images and pushes them into Amazon ECR.

After successfully pushing the images, Jenkins connects to the EC2 instances and deploys the latest Docker containers.

Finally, we use Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring application logs and infrastructure metrics."

Important: In this architecture, Jenkins handles CI/CD, EC2 hosts the Docker containers, and ECR stores the images. We are not using ECS or Fargate.
